Relational Algebra is a formal system and foundational theory for querying and manipulating relational databases. It provides a set of operations that take one or two relations (tables) as input and produce a new relation as output. This algebra forms the theoretical basis for SQL and other query languages.

Definition and Core Understanding
Relational Algebra is a procedural query language consisting of a set of operations that operate on relations (tables).
It manipulates relations (sets of tuples with the same attributes) to produce new relations.
It is closed under its operations, meaning the output of any operation is also a relation.
It provides a formal foundation for relational databases and query optimization.
Core Concepts
Term	Explanation
Relation	A table with rows (tuples) and columns (attributes).
Tuple	A single row in a relation.
Attribute	A column in a relation, representing a data field.
Schema	The structure of a relation, i.e., the set of attributes.
Domain	The set of possible values for an attribute.
Fundamental Operations of Relational Algebra
1. Selection (σ)
Filters rows based on a predicate (condition).
Syntax:
[ \sigma_{\text{condition}}(R) ]
Example: Select all employees with salary > 50000.
Output: Relation with tuples satisfying the condition.
2. Projection (π)
Extracts specific columns (attributes) from a relation.
Syntax:
[ \pi_{\text{attributes}}(R) ]
Example: Get only the names and salaries of employees.
Output: Relation with specified attributes.
3. Union (∪)
Combines tuples from two relations with the same schema, removing duplicates.
Syntax:
[ R \cup S ]
Both relations must be union-compatible (same attributes and domains).
4. Set Difference (−)
Tuples in relation R but not in S.
Syntax:
[ R - S ]
5. Cartesian Product (×)
Combines every tuple of R with every tuple of S.
Syntax:
[ R \times S ]
Resulting relation has attributes from both R and S.
6. Rename (ρ)
Renames the output relation or attributes for clarity.
Syntax:
[ \rho_{newName}(R) ]
7. Join (⨝)
Combines tuples from two relations based on a join condition.
Most common is the natural join, which joins on attributes with the same name.
Syntax:
[ R \bowtie S ]
Derived Operations
Intersection (∩):
[ R \cap S = R - (R - S) ]

Division (÷): Used for queries like "Find all X such that for all Y in S, (X,Y) is in R."

